# Obsidian session log

This project is connected to an Obsidian vault over MCP (the `obsidian` server in `.mcp.json`).

## At the start of a session

- If the user's first message names a topic, date, or alias, go straight to that note with
  `search_query` (match on `frontmatter.summary`, `frontmatter.aliases`, or a path glob),
  read it, and continue from there. Don't present a list.
- If they don't, use `search_query` to pull the most recent few notes under
  `Claude Code/Sessions/<project>/`, show their `frontmatter.summary` newest-first, and ask
  which to resume or whether to start something new.
- **Never search via `Claude Code/Sessions/<project>/_index.md`.** It's a Dataview index for
  humans to read. Dataview renders at display time, so reading the file over MCP returns the
  query text, not results. Always search frontmatter directly with `search_query`.

## When to save

Save at whichever comes first. Don't wait to be asked.

- The session reaches a natural end.
- **The topic changes** — the previous thread wrapped up and a different subject starts. Save the
  note for the finished topic at that point, and start the next topic as a new note.

## Where to save

`Claude Code/Sessions/<project>/YYYY-MM-DD - <topic>.md`, following the template at
`Claude Code/Templates/session-log.md`.

**Always fill in `summary` and `aliases`.** These are the only fields search can match on next
time. `summary` is one line on what happened; `aliases` are the other ways the user might refer
to it later. A note saved with an empty `summary` is a note you will never find again.

Several topics in one day means several notes — vary the `<topic>` part.

Also append a link to each saved note under the `## Claude Code` heading of that day's daily note.

